{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table exposes a few small helper functions for authoring table configuration with better TypeScript inference. They do not add behavior by themselves. Instead, they help TypeScript preserve the relationship between your feature set, row type, column definitions, table options, and named function registries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most common helpers are:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" - defines the static feature set and feature-related slots for a table"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" - preserves inference when composing reusable table options"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper"},{"type":"text","value":" - creates strongly typed column definitions"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These helpers are exported from every framework adapter and from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/table-core"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tablefeatures","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" to declare which features a table uses. Features are opt-in in v9, so the object you pass to this helper determines which feature APIs and state slices exist on the table, columns, rows, headers, and cells."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a basic table that only needs core behavior, pass an empty object:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add feature objects and row model factories when your table needs extra behavior:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the full built-in registries ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":") can still be spread into these slots, but they are deprecated because they put every built-in function in your bundle. Register only the functions you use under their conventional string keys, or pass functions directly to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" column options with no registration at all. String keys, including the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'auto'"},{"type":"text","value":", only resolve functions that are registered."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" stable. In most apps, define it outside your component or in shared table setup code. Since "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"typeof features"},{"type":"text","value":" is used throughout the table's types, stable features also make it easier to reuse column helpers, column definitions, and shared options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-goes-in-tablefeatures","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What goes in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" is the static configuration point for things that are stitched into a table before it is created:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature objects such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnFilteringFeature"},{"type":"text","value":", or custom features"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row model factories such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSortedRowModel()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFilteredRowModel()"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Function registries such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-only slots such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", usually declared with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metaHelper"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metaHelper()"},{"type":"text","value":" is useful when declaring type-only meta slots in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":". It is equivalent to writing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{} as MyMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", but makes the intent clearer and avoids lint rules accidentally removing the type assertion:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  metaHelper,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype MyTableMeta = {\n  updateData: (rowIndex: number, columnId: string, value: unknown) => void\n}\n\ntype MyColumnMeta = {\n  align?: 'left' | 'right'\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableMeta: metaHelper<MyTableMeta>(),\n  columnMeta: metaHelper<MyColumnMeta>(),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more detail on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metaHelper"},{"type":"text","value":", see the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-and-column-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table and Column Meta Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Function registry keys become valid string references in table and column options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  createColumnHelper,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  rank: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    byRank: (rowA, rowB, columnId) => {\n      return rowA.getValue<number>(columnId) - rowB.getValue<number>(columnId)\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('rank', {\n    sortFn: 'byRank',\n  }),\n])","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more detail on row model slots and function registries, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"./row-models","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Models Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tableoptions","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" when you want to compose reusable table option objects while keeping TanStack Table's generic inference intact. The helper returns the same object at runtime; its value is in the TypeScript overloads."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is useful for shared defaults:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { tableFeatures, tableOptions, useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\nconst sharedOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  defaultColumn: {\n    minSize: 80,\n    maxSize: 400,\n  },\n  debugTable: true,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...sharedOptions,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is also useful when a wrapper or table factory supplies required options later. For example, a reusable option fragment can omit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" and still keep the remaining table option types connected to the same feature set and row type:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  tableOptions,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst sortableDefaults = tableOptions<typeof features, Person>({\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [{ id: 'lastName', desc: false }],\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  ...sortableDefaults,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" for reusable configuration. For one-off table setup, passing the object directly to your adapter's table creation function is usually enough."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"createcolumnhelper","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates a small set of utilities for authoring column definitions:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"accessor"},{"type":"text","value":" creates data columns from an accessor key or accessor function"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"display"},{"type":"text","value":" creates display-only columns such as actions or selection controls"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" creates parent columns that contain nested columns"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" wraps arrays of column definitions to preserve each column's individual value type"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The helper is typed by both your feature set and row type:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createColumnHelper, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n  }),\n  columnHelper.accessor((row) => row.lastName, {\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: 'Last Name',\n  }),\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: (info) => `Actions for ${info.row.id}`,\n  }),\n])","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At runtime, the column helper only returns column definition objects. Its main purpose is to infer accessor values, feature-specific column options, and nested column arrays more precisely than plain object literals can in some TypeScript scenarios."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column definitions have their own guide with the full details on accessor columns, display columns, grouping columns, IDs, and rendering. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./column-defs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Defs Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-use-each-helper","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Use Each Helper"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" for every v9 table. Even an empty "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({})"},{"type":"text","value":" call clearly declares that the table only uses core features."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" when options are shared, composed, or supplied in multiple steps. Skip it when the options object is only passed directly to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTable"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectTable"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableController"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructTable"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper()"},{"type":"text","value":" when you want strong column inference, especially for accessor values, custom feature options, and nested column groups. Plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ColumnDef"},{"type":"text","value":" objects are still valid when they are easier for your use case."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"tablefeatures","text":"tableFeatures","level":2},{"id":"what-goes-in-tablefeatures","text":"What goes in tableFeatures","level":3},{"id":"tableoptions","text":"tableOptions","level":2},{"id":"createcolumnhelper","text":"createColumnHelper","level":2},{"id":"when-to-use-each-helper","text":"When to Use Each Helper","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Table exposes a few small helper functions for authoring table configuration with better TypeScript inference. They do not add behavior by themselves. Instead, they help TypeScript preserve the relationship between your feature set, row type, column definitions, table options, and named function registries.\n\nThe most common helpers are:\n\n- `tableFeatures` - defines the static feature set and feature-related slots for a table\n- `tableOptions` - preserves inference when composing reusable table options\n- `createColumnHelper` - creates strongly typed column definitions\n\nThese helpers are exported from every framework adapter and from `@tanstack/table-core`.\n\n## `tableFeatures`\n\nUse `tableFeatures()` to declare which features a table uses. Features are opt-in in v9, so the object you pass to this helper determines which feature APIs and state slices exist on the table, columns, rows, headers, and cells.\n\nFor a basic table that only needs core behavior, pass an empty object:\n\n```ts\nimport { tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n```\n\nAdd feature objects and row model factories when your table needs extra behavior:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> the full built-in registries (`filterFns`, `sortFns`, `aggregationFns`) can still be spread into these slots, but they are deprecated because they put every built-in function in your bundle. Register only the functions you use under their conventional string keys, or pass functions directly to the `filterFn`, `sortFn`, and `aggregationFn` column options with no registration at all. String keys, including the default `'auto'`, only resolve functions that are registered.\n\nKeep `features` stable. In most apps, define it outside your component or in shared table setup code. Since `typeof features` is used throughout the table's types, stable features also make it easier to reuse column helpers, column definitions, and shared options.\n\n### What goes in `tableFeatures`\n\n`tableFeatures()` is the static configuration point for things that are stitched into a table before it is created:\n\n- Feature objects such as `rowSortingFeature`, `columnFilteringFeature`, or custom features\n- Row model factories such as `createSortedRowModel()` and `createFilteredRowModel()`\n- Function registries such as `filterFns`, `sortFns`, and `aggregationFns`\n- Type-only slots such as `tableMeta` and `columnMeta`, usually declared with `metaHelper`\n\n`metaHelper()` is useful when declaring type-only meta slots in `tableFeatures()`. It is equivalent to writing `{} as MyMeta`, but makes the intent clearer and avoids lint rules accidentally removing the type assertion:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  metaHelper,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype MyTableMeta = {\n  updateData: (rowIndex: number, columnId: string, value: unknown) => void\n}\n\ntype MyColumnMeta = {\n  align?: 'left' | 'right'\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableMeta: metaHelper<MyTableMeta>(),\n  columnMeta: metaHelper<MyColumnMeta>(),\n})\n```\n\nFor more detail on `tableMeta`, `columnMeta`, and `metaHelper`, see the [Table and Column Meta Guide](./table-and-column-meta).\n\nFunction registry keys become valid string references in table and column options:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createColumnHelper,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  rank: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    byRank: (rowA, rowB, columnId) => {\n      return rowA.getValue<number>(columnId) - rowB.getValue<number>(columnId)\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('rank', {\n    sortFn: 'byRank',\n  }),\n])\n```\n\nFor more detail on row model slots and function registries, see the [Row Models Guide](./row-models).\n\n## `tableOptions`\n\nUse `tableOptions()` when you want to compose reusable table option objects while keeping TanStack Table's generic inference intact. The helper returns the same object at runtime; its value is in the TypeScript overloads.\n\nThis is useful for shared defaults:\n\n```ts\nimport { tableFeatures, tableOptions, useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\nconst sharedOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  defaultColumn: {\n    minSize: 80,\n    maxSize: 400,\n  },\n  debugTable: true,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...sharedOptions,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\nIt is also useful when a wrapper or table factory supplies required options later. For example, a reusable option fragment can omit `data`, `columns`, or `features` and still keep the remaining table option types connected to the same feature set and row type:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  tableOptions,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst sortableDefaults = tableOptions<typeof features, Person>({\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [{ id: 'lastName', desc: false }],\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  ...sortableDefaults,\n})\n```\n\nUse `tableOptions()` for reusable configuration. For one-off table setup, passing the object directly to your adapter's table creation function is usually enough.\n\n## `createColumnHelper`\n\n`createColumnHelper()` creates a small set of utilities for authoring column definitions:\n\n- `accessor` creates data columns from an accessor key or accessor function\n- `display` creates display-only columns such as actions or selection controls\n- `group` creates parent columns that contain nested columns\n- `columns` wraps arrays of column definitions to preserve each column's individual value type\n\nThe helper is typed by both your feature set and row type:\n\n```ts\nimport { createColumnHelper, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n  }),\n  columnHelper.accessor((row) => row.lastName, {\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: 'Last Name',\n  }),\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: (info) => `Actions for ${info.row.id}`,\n  }),\n])\n```\n\nAt runtime, the column helper only returns column definition objects. Its main purpose is to infer accessor values, feature-specific column options, and nested column arrays more precisely than plain object literals can in some TypeScript scenarios.\n\nColumn definitions have their own guide with the full details on accessor columns, display columns, grouping columns, IDs, and rendering. See the [Column Defs Guide](./column-defs).\n\n## When to Use Each Helper\n\nUse `tableFeatures()` for every v9 table. Even an empty `tableFeatures({})` call clearly declares that the table only uses core features.\n\nUse `tableOptions()` when options are shared, composed, or supplied in multiple steps. Skip it when the options object is only passed directly to `useTable`, `createTable`, `injectTable`, `TableController`, or `constructTable`.\n\nUse `createColumnHelper()` when you want strong column inference, especially for accessor values, custom feature options, and nested column groups. Plain `ColumnDef` objects are still valid when they are easier for your use case.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Helpers Guide\n---\n\nTanStack Table exposes a few small helper functions for authoring table configuration with better TypeScript inference. They do not add behavior by themselves. Instead, they help TypeScript preserve the relationship between your feature set, row type, column definitions, table options, and named function registries.\n\nThe most common helpers are:\n\n- `tableFeatures` - defines the static feature set and feature-related slots for a table\n- `tableOptions` - preserves inference when composing reusable table options\n- `createColumnHelper` - creates strongly typed column definitions\n\nThese helpers are exported from every framework adapter and from `@tanstack/table-core`.\n\n## `tableFeatures`\n\nUse `tableFeatures()` to declare which features a table uses. Features are opt-in in v9, so the object you pass to this helper determines which feature APIs and state slices exist on the table, columns, rows, headers, and cells.\n\nFor a basic table that only needs core behavior, pass an empty object:\n\n```ts\nimport { tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n```\n\nAdd feature objects and row model factories when your table needs extra behavior:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> the full built-in registries (`filterFns`, `sortFns`, `aggregationFns`) can still be spread into these slots, but they are deprecated because they put every built-in function in your bundle. Register only the functions you use under their conventional string keys, or pass functions directly to the `filterFn`, `sortFn`, and `aggregationFn` column options with no registration at all. String keys, including the default `'auto'`, only resolve functions that are registered.\n\nKeep `features` stable. In most apps, define it outside your component or in shared table setup code. Since `typeof features` is used throughout the table's types, stable features also make it easier to reuse column helpers, column definitions, and shared options.\n\n### What goes in `tableFeatures`\n\n`tableFeatures()` is the static configuration point for things that are stitched into a table before it is created:\n\n- Feature objects such as `rowSortingFeature`, `columnFilteringFeature`, or custom features\n- Row model factories such as `createSortedRowModel()` and `createFilteredRowModel()`\n- Function registries such as `filterFns`, `sortFns`, and `aggregationFns`\n- Type-only slots such as `tableMeta` and `columnMeta`, usually declared with `metaHelper`\n\n`metaHelper()` is useful when declaring type-only meta slots in `tableFeatures()`. It is equivalent to writing `{} as MyMeta`, but makes the intent clearer and avoids lint rules accidentally removing the type assertion:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  metaHelper,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype MyTableMeta = {\n  updateData: (rowIndex: number, columnId: string, value: unknown) => void\n}\n\ntype MyColumnMeta = {\n  align?: 'left' | 'right'\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableMeta: metaHelper<MyTableMeta>(),\n  columnMeta: metaHelper<MyColumnMeta>(),\n})\n```\n\nFor more detail on `tableMeta`, `columnMeta`, and `metaHelper`, see the [Table and Column Meta Guide](./table-and-column-meta).\n\nFunction registry keys become valid string references in table and column options:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createColumnHelper,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  rank: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    byRank: (rowA, rowB, columnId) => {\n      return rowA.getValue<number>(columnId) - rowB.getValue<number>(columnId)\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('rank', {\n    sortFn: 'byRank',\n  }),\n])\n```\n\nFor more detail on row model slots and function registries, see the [Row Models Guide](./row-models).\n\n## `tableOptions`\n\nUse `tableOptions()` when you want to compose reusable table option objects while keeping TanStack Table's generic inference intact. The helper returns the same object at runtime; its value is in the TypeScript overloads.\n\nThis is useful for shared defaults:\n\n```ts\nimport { tableFeatures, tableOptions, useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\nconst sharedOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  defaultColumn: {\n    minSize: 80,\n    maxSize: 400,\n  },\n  debugTable: true,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...sharedOptions,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\nIt is also useful when a wrapper or table factory supplies required options later. For example, a reusable option fragment can omit `data`, `columns`, or `features` and still keep the remaining table option types connected to the same feature set and row type:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  tableOptions,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst sortableDefaults = tableOptions<typeof features, Person>({\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [{ id: 'lastName', desc: false }],\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  ...sortableDefaults,\n})\n```\n\nUse `tableOptions()` for reusable configuration. For one-off table setup, passing the object directly to your adapter's table creation function is usually enough.\n\n## `createColumnHelper`\n\n`createColumnHelper()` creates a small set of utilities for authoring column definitions:\n\n- `accessor` creates data columns from an accessor key or accessor function\n- `display` creates display-only columns such as actions or selection controls\n- `group` creates parent columns that contain nested columns\n- `columns` wraps arrays of column definitions to preserve each column's individual value type\n\nThe helper is typed by both your feature set and row type:\n\n```ts\nimport { createColumnHelper, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n  }),\n  columnHelper.accessor((row) => row.lastName, {\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: 'Last Name',\n  }),\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: (info) => `Actions for ${info.row.id}`,\n  }),\n])\n```\n\nAt runtime, the column helper only returns column definition objects. Its main purpose is to infer accessor values, feature-specific column options, and nested column arrays more precisely than plain object literals can in some TypeScript scenarios.\n\nColumn definitions have their own guide with the full details on accessor columns, display columns, grouping columns, IDs, and rendering. See the [Column Defs Guide](./column-defs).\n\n## When to Use Each Helper\n\nUse `tableFeatures()` for every v9 table. Even an empty `tableFeatures({})` call clearly declares that the table only uses core features.\n\nUse `tableOptions()` when options are shared, composed, or supplied in multiple steps. Skip it when the options object is only passed directly to `useTable`, `createTable`, `injectTable`, `TableController`, or `constructTable`.\n\nUse `createColumnHelper()` when you want strong column inference, especially for accessor values, custom feature options, and nested column groups. Plain `ColumnDef` objects are still valid when they are easier for your use case.\n"}
